| `vectorops/__init__.py` | re-exports `dotproduct`, sets `__version__ = "0.1.0"` |
| `vectorops/_core.py` | implementation |
| `pyproject.toml` | setuptools metadata, name `vectorops`, version `0.1.0` |
| `tests/test_dotproduct.py` | 9 pytest tests |
| `dist/` + `packages/` | wheel and sdist (byte-identical copies) |
| `serve.sh` | starts/restarts the index on 8080 |
| `stop_server.py` | portable port stopper (`pkill`/`lsof`/`fuser` absent here) |
| `verify.sh` | full end-to-end check |

## Verification actually run

```
$ ./verify.sh; echo "VERIFY_EXIT=$?"
== 1/5 unit tests ==   9 passed
== 2/5 build ==        vectorops-0.1.0-py3-none-any.whl, vectorops-0.1.0.tar.gz
== 3/5 publish ==      copied into packages/
== 4/5 index ==        index serving on http://localhost:8080/simple
== 5/5 install ==
Downloading http://localhost:8080/packages/vectorops-0.1.0-py3-none-any.whl (2.4 kB)
Successfully installed vectorops-0.1.0
OK: dotproduct([1,1],[0,1]) == 1
OK: imported from .../site-packages/vectorops/__init__.py
== all checks passed ==
VERIFY_EXIT=0
```

The install used exactly `pip install --index-url http://localhost:8080/simple vectorops==0.1.0` into a throwaway venv, and the assertion ran from `/tmp` so the source tree couldn't shadow the installed copy. I also confirmed the sdist path installs (`--no-binary :all:`), and that `vectorops==0.2.0` correctly fails with `from versions: 0.1.0` — proving pip is really talking to this index.

## Two things worth flagging

**My own test expectation was wrong, not the code.** I asserted `-1 == dotproduct([1,2],[-1,-1])`; the true answer is `-3` (confirmed via `compute`). I corrected the *test*, since the implementation matched the specification — the reverse order would have encoded a bug.

**A real bug in my first `serve.sh`.** The log showed `OSError: [Errno 98] Address already in use` — my `pkill -f` restart was silently a no-op because this image has no `pkill`, no `fuser`, no `lsof`. The old server kept serving and `curl` still returned 200, so the verification "passed" while testing stale artifacts. Fixed by writing `stop_server.py`, which finds the listening socket via `/proc/net/tcp` inode → `/proc/<pid>/fd`. `serve.sh` now refuses to start when the port is busy unless given `--restart`, so this failure mode is loud rather than masked. Re-ran the whole chain afterwards; the log is clean.

I also mutation-tested the suite: replacing `x*y` with `x+y` fails 6 tests, and removing the length check fails the `ValueError` test — so the tests bite rather than passing by construction.

The server is still live and detached (log at `.gnomon-jobs/pypiserver.log`, `simple index: 200`). It is unauthenticated (`-a . -P .`), which is fine for localhost but should not be bound to a public interface.

